course: everyday
lesson: 227
title: Respect for first peoples, older people and people with disabilities
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Respect means treating people as they would want to be treated: with patience, kindness and without staring, teasing or taking over.
explain: Older people may find bending, carrying or hurrying hard, and some forget things because memory can get weaker with age. A little patience and help go a long way.
explain: A disability can be easy to see, like a wheelchair, a white cane or a prosthetic leg, or invisible, like diabetes, a heart problem, autism or ADHD. People with disabilities want to be talked to and treated like everyone else.
explain: Every land has its first peoples: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in Australia, Māori in New Zealand, Native American and First Nations peoples in North America. When their cultures are talked about, listen and learn.
explain: Example: a boy in Auckland sees his blind neighbour at the bus stop. He says «Hello, Mr Reid, it's Tom from next door», and does not pat the guide dog, because the dog is working.
fact: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, Māori and Native American peoples are the first peoples of their lands, and their cultures deserve respect.
fact: Bending down can be hard or painful for older people, so pick up what they drop.
fact: Offer to help an older neighbour carry heavy shopping, with your parent nearby.
fact: Do not rush or push past an older person walking slowly, because they might fall if pushed or startled.
fact: When an older relative does not understand a phone or game, show them slowly and patiently.
fact: Memory can get weaker with age, and some older people have dementia, an illness that damages memory, so answer repeated questions kindly.
fact: Talk to a wheelchair user, not to the person with them, and never lean on or push the chair without asking.
fact: When you speak to a blind person, say who you are, and never distract a working guide dog.
fact: When a classmate stammers, wait patiently and do not finish their words.
fact: Autistic people experience the world differently and may find loud noise or sudden changes hard.
fact: ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not bad behaviour.
fact: Many disabilities are invisible, so believe someone who says they need a snack or a rest.
fact: Mean words about disability hurt, so do not use them and say they are not okay.
fact: A prosthetic arm or leg is a tool that helps someone, like glasses, so do not stare.
exercise: Your class learns about Aboriginal culture from a visiting elder. What should you do? | Listen respectfully and learn | Chat with your friends at the back | Say they are just old stories anyway | Ask when the real lesson starts
exercise: Who are Māori in New Zealand? | The first people of those islands | People who arrived there last year | A group of tourists from Europe | A type of sports team
exercise: An older lady drops her glove in the shop. What should you do? | Pick it up and give it to her | Leave it, as she will notice herself | Point at it and walk on | Take it to the lost property later
exercise: Why is picking things up for older people kind? | Bending down can be hard or painful for them | Older people are not allowed to bend down in public | It is a rule in all shops | They will give you a reward
exercise: Your older neighbour is struggling with heavy bags. What is a good thing to do? | Ask «Can I help you carry that?» with your parent nearby | Take the bags out of her hands without asking | Go into her house alone to help unpack | Pretend not to notice, so that she is not embarrassed in the street
exercise: An old man walks slowly in front of you on a narrow path. What should you do? | Wait patiently or pass carefully when there is room | Squeeze past him quickly | Tell him to hurry up | Shout so he moves to the side
exercise: Grandpa cannot work out how to send a photo on his phone. What should you do? | Show him slowly and patiently | Do it for him and sigh loudly | Tell him he is too old for phones | Laugh and tell your friends
exercise: Your great-aunt asks your name for the third time today. What is right? | Answer kindly, since memory can weaken with age | Tell her she already asked twice | Make fun of her to your cousins | Ignore the question this time
exercise: You meet a woman in a wheelchair with her friend. Who should you talk to when you speak to her? | The woman herself | Her friend, who can answer for her | Nobody, it is rude to speak to her | Whoever is closest to you
exercise: Why should you not lean on someone's wheelchair? | It is part of their personal space | It might make the chair go faster | Wheelchairs are too dirty to touch | It is only fine if they are asleep
exercise: You see a man with a guide dog waiting to cross. What should you NOT do? | Pat or call the dog | Say who you are if you speak to him | Ask if he would like help | Let him decide whether he wants help
exercise: A classmate stutters (stammers) when reading aloud. How can you help? | Wait patiently and let him finish | Finish his words for him to save everyone time | Tell him to speak faster | Laugh so he feels relaxed
exercise: An autistic classmate covers her ears during the fire drill. What should you do? | Stay calm and be patient with her | Tell her she is being silly | Make loud noises to help her get used to it | Tell everyone to look at her
exercise: A boy with ADHD keeps wriggling in his chair. What is the fair way to think about it? | It is how his brain works, not naughtiness | He is being naughty on purpose to disturb the lesson | He should be kept away from the class | He wants attention and should be ignored
exercise: A girl says she needs to eat a snack right now because of her diabetes. What should you do? | Believe her and help if you can | Say she does not look ill | Tell her snacks are only for break time | Ask her to prove it first
exercise: Someone uses a mean word about disability in the playground. What should you do? | Not use it, and say it is not okay | Use it too, so that you fit in with the others | Laugh, since it is only a word | Tell them a worse word
exercise: A new girl has a prosthetic leg. How should you treat her? | Normally, without staring | Ask to touch the leg straight away | Keep her out of running games | Feel sorry for her all the time
test: A girl in Perth hears an Aboriginal storyteller at a school assembly. Why does his culture deserve special respect? | His people are the first peoples of that land | The stories are only there for entertainment, like a film | The school will test her on it | It is polite to listen to any visitor once
test: A boy runs past an old woman on the stairs and she wobbles. Why was that dangerous? | She might fall if startled or pushed | Old people never mind being rushed past | Running on stairs is only dangerous for children | Nothing, as long as he did not touch her
test: Your grandma keeps telling the same story about her garden. What is the kind thing to do? | Listen kindly, as memory can weaken with age | Tell her she is boring | Walk away while she is talking | Tell her to write it down instead, so she stops repeating it
test: At a café, the waiter asks a wheelchair user's friend «What would she like?». What would have been better? | Asking the wheelchair user herself | Asking the friend again, more loudly and slowly | Choosing something for her | Bringing the menu to the friend
test: A blind woman is at the bus stop. You want to tell her the bus is coming. What should you do first? | Say who you are | Take her arm and lead her | Clap loudly to get her attention | Pat her guide dog first
test: A classmate says «I need to sit down, I have a heart condition», but he looks fine. What is true? | Many disabilities cannot be seen, so believe him | He must be lying, because he looks perfectly well to everyone | Only people in wheelchairs need rests | He should ask the teacher to check first
test: A boy keeps finishing his friend's sentences because his friend stammers. Why is that unhelpful? | His friend knows what he wants to say and needs time | It makes his friend speak more clearly | It is the best way to help stammering | It saves his friend from embarrassment
test: Which is most like a prosthetic leg? | A pair of glasses, a tool that helps someone | A costume someone wears for a school play | A toy robot leg | A plaster cast for a broken bone that is healing
test: A class party has loud music and an autistic classmate looks upset. What is a kind idea? | Ask an adult if there is a quieter place she can go | Turn the music up so she joins in | Tell her to stop making a fuss | Leave her alone and keep dancing
test: kid in my class stammers, takes ages to talk. should i finish his words to help | No, wait patiently and let him finish | Yes, it saves him time | Yes, and say them louder | Only if you guess right
test: lady's guide dog is SO cute, can i pat it | No, never distract a working guide dog | Yes, guide dogs love strangers patting them | Yes, if you're quick | Only if you give it a treat
test: grandad asked me the same thing 3 times today. annoying. what do i say | Answer him kindly again | Tell him he already asked | Ignore him the third time | Laugh about it and walk away
test: friend's in a wheelchair, can i push her to the playground for fun? | Only if she says yes when you ask | Yes, push her fast for fun | Yes, wheelchairs are made to be pushed | Ask her helper, not her
explain: An older man in Liverpool was knocked over by children running in a supermarket; he broke his hip and was in hospital for weeks.
explain: A boy parked his bike across a ramp; a woman in a wheelchair could not get to the doctor's door and missed her appointment.
fact: Falls are a major cause of serious injury in older people, and a broken hip can take months to heal.
fact: Blocking ramps, lifts and wide parking spaces can stop people with disabilities getting where they need to go.
test: race ya down the aisle | No, you could knock someone over, walk | Yes, nobody's in the way | Yes, if you're quick | Only if you shout first
test: Why is a broken hip so serious for an older person? | It can take months to heal and stop them walking | Hips never break | It heals in a day | It only hurts a bit
test: gonna leave my bike on the ramp, only 5 mins | No, people in wheelchairs need the ramp | Yes, it's quick | Yes, if it's tidy | Only by the door
test: did u hear an old man got knocked over by a kid running past in the shop and broke his hip, he's still in hospital months later. real lesson? | Don't rush past older people; falls can injure them badly | Old people should stay home | Only shove gently when in a hurry | Hips heal in a day or two
test: my mate says that girl with autism is just being dramatic when she covers her ears at assembly. true? | No, loud noise can be really hard for autistic people | Yes, she wants attention | Yes, she could stop if she tried | No, she's just bored of assembly
test: apparently that kid in year 4 only pretends he needs snacks cos of a 'condition', everyone's saying he's faking. pass it on? | No, many disabilities are invisible; believe him | Yes, so the teacher finally stops giving him all those snacks | Yes, if you're sure he's faking | Yes, it's only fair
exam: Identify the appropriate manner of addressing a wheelchair user accompanied by another person. | One speaks directly to the wheelchair user | One speaks only to the companion | One speaks loudly and slowly to the companion | One pushes the chair while speaking
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises ADHD? | It is a difference in how the brain works, not bad behaviour | It is bad behaviour that ceases with punishment | It is an illness caught from others | It occurs only in adults
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of a working guide dog. | It is never distracted | It may be stroked if gentle | It may be fed treats freely | It is to be called by its name
exam: Ascertain the appropriate response to an older relative with dementia who repeats a question. | To answer kindly each time | To refuse to answer a second time | To point out the repetition crossly | To leave the room
